  year={2026},
  volume={61},
  number={10},
  pages={5224-5224},
  abstract={In the above article [1], Section IV-B models the noise figure (NF) of the architecture. The analysis and the simulation conclude that its NF under idealized matching conditions with $R_{sw} = 0$ ([1, Fig. 4(c)]) is the same as that of an ideal $N$ -path filter without the matching network. However, due to the presence of the charge-sharing switched-capacitor bank $C_{N}$ , the noise folding during the charge-sharing phase between $C_{N}$ and $C_{p}$ also needs to be considered. The Spectre RF simulation results with PNOISE using its “full-spectrum” settings are shown in [1, Fig. 12]. This setting does not completely capture the noise-folding from far-off sidebands, particularly for very small on-resistance of the switches. Due to this, the plot in [1, Fig. 12] shows $\approx 1$ dB-NF for $R_{sw}$ of few tens of m $\Omega $ . To capture the complete effect, the number of harmonics in the PNOISE simulation needs to be increased to >1000 (for the schematic in [1, Fig. 4(c)]) when the time-constant associated withthe charge-sharing path is less than 10 ps. We simulated the same architecture for different values of $R_{sw}$ (while ensuring the same $S_{11}$ across the $R_{sw}$ range), with the number of sidebands increased to >1000 for various levels of input match ( $S_{11}$ ). Fig. 1(a) shows the NF with respect to $R_{sw}$ under idealized matching conditions using the aforementioned setup. To ensure that the architecture retains the same level of input match, even with changing $R_{sw}$ , the value of $C_{p}$ and the switching capacitor, $C_{N}$ , were appropriately modified. Fig. 1(b) shows the simulated NF for two different levels of $S_{11}$ .Fig. 1.Simulated NF of the architecture of [1, Fig. 4(c)]. (a) Idealized matching condition. (b) Different levels of $S_{11}$ . The NF plot in [1, Fig. 12] was generated by varying $R_{sw}$ while keeping $C_{p}$ and $C_{N}$ the same. This naturally changes $S_{11}$ as $R_{sw}$ is swept, but keeps it smaller than −10 dB for the range of $R_{sw}$ . We simulated the architecture using this setup and compared the results of the updated PNOISE setting with those reported in [1, Fig. 12]. The comparative plots are shown in Fig. 2. Note that the difference in the results between the two settings is the largest for very small ( $\lt 1~\Omega $ ) $R_{sw}$ . For $R_{sw} \gt$ $ 1~\Omega $ the full-spectrum setting of PNOISE better captures the noise folding from the far-off sidebands for the schematic of [1] and the plots tend to converge.Fig. 2.Comparison between the NF plots of the architecture of [1, Fig. 4(c)] using the updated settings and the “full-spectrum” settings for various values of $R_{sw}$ (similar value of S11 for both cases).},
  keywords={},
  doi={10.1109/JSSC.2026.3717899},
  ISSN={1558-173X},
  month={Oct},}

