  number={10},
  pages={5383-5395},
  abstract={This article presents a time-modulated variable-gain amplifier (VGA) employing a clock-sampling topology governed by a duty-cycle control (DCC) loop. By modulating the effective operation time of the amplifier rather than altering its RF bias conditions, for precise tuning of the clock duty cycle, enabling phase consistency at millimeter-wave frequencies. The proposed DCC loop provides precise and stable DCC, enabling robust time-domain gain modulation and mitigating phase perturbations typically associated with conventional VGA architectures. Implemented in 65-nm CMOS technology, the VGA consumes 4.9–103 mW from a 1-V supply, depending on the gain setting, and occupies a core area of  $1150\times 280~\mu $ m. Measurements demonstrate a 30-dB continuous gain-control range with a 3-dB bandwidth from 24 to 30 GHz and a peak gain of 29.6 dB at 28 GHz. Across 24–29.7 GHz, the measured rms phase error ranges from 0.01° to 0.18° between the highest and lowest gain modes, validating the phase stability of the proposed approach. In addition, an output 1-dB (OP1dB) compression point of 12.5 dBm is achieved, indicating robust large-signal performance. The proposed time-modulated VGA is well-suited for scalable millimeter-wave phased-array systems requiring wide-range gain control with minimal phase distortion.},
  keywords={Solids;Radio frequency;Technology;Clocks;Gain;Timing;Amplifiers;Tuning;Transceivers;Awards;Duty-cycle control (DCC);low phase variation;millimeter-wave (mm-Wave);phase-invariant;time modulation;variable-gain amplifier (VGA)},
  doi={10.1109/JSSC.2026.3695362},
