  volume={61},
  number={10},
  pages={5472-5484},
  abstract={This work presents a two-step SAR-assisted hybrid 2-1 multistage noise-shaping (MASH) incremental analog-to-digital converter (I–ADC) that combines a hybrid continuous-time–discrete-time (CT–DT) delta–sigma modulator (DSM) with a noise-shaping SAR (NS-SAR) ADC. A two-step operation is employed to enhance power efficiency by disabling the CT stage during the second step (STEP2). A 4-bit SAR quantizer (QTZ) is reused across both stages, enabling a compact implementation. An automatic inter-stage gain selection (AIGS) technique is proposed to further enhance SQNR by adaptively selecting the inter-stage gain (ISG) based on the residue magnitude, allowing large ISG values without second-stage saturation. The resulting architecture provides second-order noise shaping in the first-step conversion and additional first-order shaping in the second-step conversion. Fabricated in 65-nm CMOS, the prototype achieves a 96.7-dB SNDR and a 100.7-dB DR for a 160-kHz bandwidth (BW) while consuming 1.61 mW at a 1.5-V supply and 16.64-MHz sampling frequency. The design achieves a Schreier figure of merit (FoMS) of 176.7 dB, demonstrating the effectiveness of the proposed architecture with the AIGS technique.},
  keywords={Solids;Specific absorption rate;Synthetic aperture radar;Noise shaping;Quantization (signal);Gain;Noise;Multi-stage noise shaping;Shape;Timing;Delta–sigma modulators (DSMs);high-resolution;hybrid continuous-time–discrete-time (CT–DT);incremental analog-to-digital converters (I–ADCs);inter-stage gain (ISG);multistage noise-shaping (MASH);noise-shaping SAR (NS-SAR);reconfiguration;sensor interface},
  doi={10.1109/JSSC.2026.3706374},
