  year={2026},
  volume={61},
  number={10},
  pages={5496-5509},
  abstract={This article presents a 436–472 GHz four-element intermediate-frequency (IF) beamforming phased-array receiver fabricated in 65-nm CMOS, targeting scalable terahertz (THz) phased-array systems. The proposed receiver adopts a two-step mixing scheme to reduce the area overhead of the IF beamforming implementation, thereby enhancing the array scalability at THz frequencies. Each receive channel integrates an on-chip sub-THz frequency multiplier chain to generate sufficient local oscillator (LO) excitation to drive the THz mixer. A 6-bit wideband switch-type phase shifter with an optimized topology suppresses the parasitic feedthrough, achieving a 51–66 GHz operating bandwidth. A broadband on-chip patch antenna array with a quartz superstrate achieves a simulated 3-dB gain bandwidth of 91 GHz by using multiple resonant modes. The receiver demonstrates a measured operating bandwidth of 36 GHz, a ±35° beam-steering range, and a minimum noise figure (NF) of 27.5 dB. To the best of our knowledge, this work demonstrates the first CMOS phased-array receiver front-end operating above 300 GHz, paving the way toward scalable, low-cost CMOS THz phased-array systems.},
