  journal={IEEE Journal of Solid-State Circuits}, 
  title={A 138 Gb/s D-Band 2-D Scalable Transceiver Array With On-Chip Antennas Achieving Sub-1-pJ/b Efficiency in 28-nm Bulk CMOS}, 
  year={2026},
  volume={61},
  number={10},
  pages={5510-5524},
  abstract={This article presents a D-band 2-D scalable phased array that integrates a  $2{\,}\times{\,} 2$  transceiving (TRX) radio frequency (RF) beamformer with on-chip antennas (OCAs) and a  $\times 16$  local-oscillator (LO) multiplication chain. The 2-D array scalability is demonstrated by tiling  $2{\,}\times{\,} 2$  chips into an array using a low-cost package to build a 16-element hybrid array. The overall TRX system achieves >18% instantaneous RF fractional bandwidth (BW) and adopts a superheterodyne architecture with 10–40 GHz intermediate frequency (IF) range. The scalable chip occupies  $2.115 \times 2.115$  mm2 and is designed and fabricated in 28-nm bulk CMOS technology. Dual-fed octagonal slot-loop antennas are shared between the transmitter (TX) and the receiver (RX) channels. The OCAs radiate from the chip backside after flip-chip assembly. Substrate thinning and substrate-mode cancellation techniques are applied to boost the OCA radiation efficiency to 33% at 140 GHz. The array maintains  $0.45\lambda _{o}$  spacing between on-chip elements and  $0.67\lambda _{o}$  between neighboring elements on different chips, which is limited by chip-to-chip spacing. The 16-element array module demonstrates a TX effective isotropic radiated power (EIRP) of 33 dBm. An RX noise figure (NF) of 6 dB was measured over-the-air (OTA) using the Cold Source method. Beam steering was demonstrated up to ±60° in the H-plane and ±40° in the E-plane using integrated 5-bit phase shifters. This work demonstrates 138 Gb/s OTA using 64-QAM for the overall transmit-receive link while achieving an energy efficiency of 0.33/0.73 pJ/b in RX/TX mode. In addition, the link achieves a data rate of 108 and 72 Gb/s at 1 and 4 m link range, respectively. To the best of the authors’ knowledge, this article demonstrates one of the highest reported array-level OTA data rates and the best energy efficiency (pJ/b) among other D-band arrays by a significant margin.},
  keywords={Arrays;Joining processes;Antennas;Distance measurement;Modeling;Frequency;Gain;Substrates;Radio frequency;Phased arrays;140 GHz;6G;beamformer;CMOS;D-band;mm-wave;on-chip antenna (OCA);phased array;receiver (RX);radio frequency (RF);sub-THz;transceiver;transmitter (TX);wireless communications},
