  abstract={This article presents a 300-GHz 2-D scalable  $4\times 4$  phased-array receiver (RX) with half-wavelength element spacing in 65-nm CMOS. To achieve high scalability, a hybrid local oscillator (LO)–intermediate frequency (IF) beamforming scheme based on a coupled oscillator array is proposed, together with an area-efficient aperture-coupled antenna implemented on quartz. The coupled oscillator array serves as a de-centralized compact LO source with low interference to the IF network, which is essential for enhancing phased-array scalability. In addition, it inherently provides phase-control capability, thereby reducing the area overhead of dedicated phase shifters. To guide the design of the proposed phase-control approach, the effect of nonideal LO phase progression in this scheme on beamforming performance is analyzed. The area-efficient aperture-coupled antenna employs a quartz superstrate with metallization on both surfaces. The slotted ground plane on the bottom quartz surface isolates the patch antenna from the on-chip circuits, enabling circuit integration beneath the patch and, thereby, improving phased-array scalability. The  $4\times 4$  phased-array RX achieves a steering range of ±40° in the  $H$ -plane and ±24° in the  $E$ -plane. Measurement results show an RF 3-dB bandwidth of 293–307 GHz and a minimum noise figure of 23.6 dB. The chip consumes a DC power of 1.4 W.},
