  volume={61},
  number={10},
  pages={5601-5620},
  abstract={This article introduces a CP-sampling (CS)-fusion PLL architecture that combines the advantages of PFD-CP PLLs (robust and speedy locking) and sampling PLLs (low jitter/power) in a single loop. The PFD sends pulsewidth-modulation (PWM) pulses to a merged  $\mathbf {G_{m}}$ /CP (GCP) block so that the phase error is represented by the PWM pulse during locking and the sampled voltage after lock. The locking benefit over a traditional sampling PLL relying on a frequency-locked loop (FLL) is analyzed and then verified via transistor-level simulation. The CS-fusion architecture is validated through two prototypes in silicon based on different types of PFDs and ramp generators, whose output delivers the sampled voltage ( $\mathbf {V_{s}}$ ) controlling the voltage-controlled oscillator (VCO) in the P path, while also driving a PWM GCP followed by a loop filter in the mixed P–I path. The measurement achieves −253.3-dB jitter FOM ( $\mathbf {FOM_{J}}$ ) and −248.4-dB reference FOM ( $\mathbf {FOM_{ref}}$ ) while also attaining competitive locking cycles, with an average locking speed  $4.3\times $  faster than the case disabling the CS-fusion technique ( $7.3\times $  faster if both without the aid of P path).},
  keywords={Phase locked loops;Poles and zeros;Timing;Jitter;Phase frequency detectors;Voltage;Gain;Current;Design methodology;Solids;Phase-locked loop (PLL);CP-sampling (CS)-fusion PLL;robust and speedy locking;low jitter; $\mathbf {G_{m}}$  /CP;GCP;zero-offset;reverse PFD},
