  abstract={This article presents a versatile laser diode driver (LDD) for pulsed light detection and ranging (LiDAR) systems, featuring wide tunability in both pulsewidth and intensity to support a wide range of applications and dynamic adaptation across diverse ranging scenarios. The proposed LDD comprises three core components: a power stage with eight-unit cells supporting multiple current levels, a laser diode current sampling circuit for real-time current sensing, and a digital peak current regulation loop for adaptive current control. A unique peak current sensor is designed to capture the nanosecond-width and high-amplitude peak current, with careful design consideration for high-voltage operation. The proposed driver is fabricated in 180-nm BCD technology with an on-die laser diode and silicon/ceramic capacitors to minimize parasitics. The prototype is characterized under two setups covering low-/medium- and high-power operation. Measurement results demonstrate a pulsewidth tunability from 100 ps to 100 ns and a regulated peak current range of 3–22 A. Compared with state-of-the-art designs, this work achieves the widest tunable range and is also the first design that demonstrated closed-loop current regulation in optical transient measurement.},
