  abstract={This article presents a  $2\times 56$ -Gb/s single-ended (SE) PAM-4 transceiver (TRX) with the encoder-based crosstalk cancellation (EB-XTC) technique, enabling 4-bit/symbol data transmission over a differential link. The EB-XTC scheme encodes two uncorrelated SE PAM-4 signals into a pair of seven-level orthogonal signals at the transmitter side and decodes them to PAM-4 at the receiver side, effectively canceling strong crosstalk in the closely coupled differential channel. To improve the linearity of the seven-level orthogonal signal, this work introduces a tailless push-pull (TLPP) driver with a linearity calibration technique in the bias generator, enhancing the ratio-to-level mismatch (RLM) to 0.96; moreover, the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of decoded PAM-4 is improved by 15 dB. The proposed TLPP driver and orthogonal encoder leverage the nonuniform probabilistic distribution of a seven-level orthogonal signal to reduce signaling power. The prototype chips are fabricated in 28-nm CMOS and achieve 56 Gb/s/pin with crosstalk immunity up to −2 dB and SE insertion loss (IL) compensation capability up to 35 dB with energy efficiency of 1.8 pJ/b.},
