  abstract={This article presents a transition-driven AC-coupled transceiver (TRX) optimized for low-power and compact die-to-die (D2D) interfaces. To address the limitations of conventional PMOS- and transimpedance-amplifier (TIA)-based receivers (RXs), the proposed RX architecture employs a small 130-fF coupling capacitor to capture only transition information, eliminating the need for bulky voltage reference generators (VrefGens) or high-static-current paths. DC levels are restored using PMOS and NMOS holders, tolerating long run lengths. The transition-driven inverter-based RX structure facilitates aggressive scaling of the transmitter (TX) supply voltage, contributing to significant power savings. A prototype chip implemented in a 28-nm CMOS process features a programmable three-lane on-chip interconnect with a 2- $\mu $ m pitch. The proposed design achieves 0.1514 pJ/bit and a figure-of-merit of 118.89 Tb/s/mm/pJ/bit, making it highly suitable for next-generation short-reach D2D interfaces.},
