  abstract={This article presents a 27-Gb/s/pin inverter-based delay-matched three-level pulse amplitude modulation (PAM-3) transceiver for short-reach die-to-die (D2D) interfaces. The proposed design uses two parallel, identical offset-programmable transimpedance amplifiers (TIAs) to independently generate two finely tunable PAM-3 reference levels with minimal parasitic loading, enabling multi-level signaling within a compact, energy-efficient inverter-based architecture. A pattern-selective switching-jitter compensation (PS-SWJC) technique is further proposed, which selectively removes switching jitter (SWJ) only at switching-jitter events, thereby recovering the horizontal margin that conventional SWJ compensation (SWJC) schemes cannot restore. Measurements show that the proposed PS-SWJC improves the horizontal margin from 0.25 to 0.41 UI (a 64% enhancement) and maintains a stable 0.31-UI margin even with 0.94-0.95-UI peak-to-peak recovered-clock jitter under 60-Hz, 10-MHz, and 100-MHz supply ripple, demonstrating strong robustness to power-supply-induced jitter (PSIJ). These features make the proposed architecture a robust solution for high-density, low-power chiplet and D2D interfaces. The prototype transceiver is fabricated in a 28-nm CMOS process and achieves an energy efficiency of 0.399 pJ/bit at the DQ channel and 0.55 pJ/bit including the forwarded clock channel at 27 Gb/s/pin.},
