  abstract={This article presents an incremental noise-shaping (NS) pipeline analog-to-digital converter (ADC) featuring a single-amplification-based kT/C noise cancellation technique. By pre-amplifying the frontend sampling kT/C noise onto the capacitive digital-to-analog converter (CDAC) of the backend NS successive approximation register (SAR) quantizer, the proposed architecture eliminates the repeated amplifications typically required in multi-bit SAR conversion employing conventional kT/C noise cancellation. Moreover, by preserving the residue information during the NS-SAR loop filter update, multi-cycle NS-SAR conversions can proceed continuously without additional residue generation. Together, these features allow multi-cycle multi-bit kT/C noise-canceled conversions to be performed with a single amplification, thus offering significant amplifier power and settling time savings. To further speed up the operation, two noise cancellation capacitors are rotated in a ping-pong fashion, enabling kT/C noise cancellation in the pipelined ADC. A variable-order loop filter is designed to enhance the conversion resolution. The floating inverter amplifier (FIA) is employed for both kT/C noise cancellation and residue amplification, with its linearity ensured through a two-step conversion and gain error calibrated in the background. Prototyped in 28-nm CMOS, the proposed incremental NS pipeline ADC achieves a 92.5-dB SNDR with a small input capacitor of 0.8 pF; it only consumes  $467.3~\mu $ W when running at 1.6 MS/s, leading to a superior Schreier figure of merit (FoM) of 184.8 dB.},
