  journal={IEEE Journal of Solid-State Circuits}, 
  title={A 0.38-pJ/step Pulse-Width Locked Time-Domain Wheatstone Bridge Sensor Readout IC for LIG-Based Wearable Strain Sensing System}, 
  year={2026},
  volume={61},
  number={10},
  pages={5775-5787},
  abstract={This article presents a pulse-width locked-loop (PWLL) time-domain readout integrated circuit (IC) for wearable strain-sensing systems based on a laser-induced graphene (LIG) strain sensor. A time-domain Wheatstone bridge (WhB) architecture is proposed, incorporating a duty-cycled resistor (DCR) and a voltage-controlled oscillator (VCO)-based integrator to realize a digitally intensive, energy-efficient, and highly accurate resistive sensor interface. A voltage-controlled delay line (VCDL) is employed to form the PWLL feedback path, enabling good linearity. In addition, a 10-bit delay-ratio-based pipelined time-to-digital converter (TDC) is proposed, eliminating the need for a time amplifier (TA). The prototype IC is fabricated in a 6-nm CMOS process, occupying an active area of 0.19 mm2 and consuming only 4.8  $\mu $ W. The system achieves high linearity with R2  >  0.99993 and a resistance-sensing resolution ranging from 8.5 to 4.1  $\Omega $  for conversion times of 0.1–1 ms, respectively, resulting in a figure of merit (FoM) of 0.38 pJ/step. Furthermore, an LIG strain sensor is fabricated and integrated with the proposed readout IC to demonstrate fast and accurate human-motion tracking.},
