  abstract={This article presents a 5-V input to 0.7–1.8-V output always-constant-current (ACC) hybrid DC–DC converter for high-performance computing (HPC) device applications. The introduced converter has a capacitor current path that compensates the AC component of the inductor current path to maintain the ACC condition. Thus, while reducing the DC inductor current, the proposed converter reduces the output voltage ripple and improves the transient response performance compared to the conventional hybrid DC–DC converters, due to the compensating operation of the ripple sensing amplifier (RSA) feedback network. This work was fabricated in a 180-nm CMOS process. The measurement result shows a minimum output voltage ripple of 2.5 mV and a fast load transient settling time of  $1.3~\mu $ s. Moreover, the converter achieves 93.4% peak power conversion efficiency with an inductor of  $1~\mu $ H that has a DC resistance (DCR) of 80 m $\Omega $  for a small converter volume.},
