  number={10},
  pages={5838-5852},
  abstract={This article presents a three-level dual-path (TLDP) buck converter tailored for mobile application processors (APs) with a 1.8-V input and 0.4–1.5-V output range. Conventional buck (CB) and multi-level buck (MLB) converters suffer from prohibitive DC resistance ( $R_{\mathrm {DCR}}$ ) losses in compact inductors as load current increases. Although various multi-path buck (MPB) designs have attempted to reduce inductor DC current ( $I_{\mathrm {L}}$ ) by supplementing output power delivery through flying capacitors ( $C_{\mathrm {F}}$ ), they are often constrained by restricted voltage conversion ratio (VCR) ranges and encounter severe efficiency degradation due to inrush capacitive currents ( $I_{\mathrm {CF,C}}$ ) at specific VCRs. In contrast, the proposed TLDP buck converter enables seamless operation across the full VCR range (0–1) while effectively mitigating inrush  $I_{\mathrm {CF,C}}$  surges. Specifically, at the typical  $V_{\mathrm {DD}}$  of 0.9 V, the proposed solution achieves optimal  $I_{\mathrm {L}}$ -reduction and near-zero  $I_{\mathrm {L}}$  ripple, thereby minimizing losses associated with the compact inductor and attaining superior efficiency. Fabricated in a 28-nm CMOS process, the TLDP buck chip delivers up to 2.5 A within a total area of 3.38 mm2, achieving a peak efficiency of 96.5% using a 1-mm3 inductor ( $L =240$  nH and  $R_{\mathrm {DCR}} =24$  m $\Omega $ ). In addition, it attains competitive performance at the target VCR of 0.5, achieving an efficiency of 96.1% at 0.118 A/mm2 and delivering a high current density of 0.740 A/mm2 with 88.6% efficiency.},
